The batch progress tracking for 'update' operations with `updateMany` has the same issue as create operations - it assumes all-or-nothing behavior. At lines 391-393, the code sets `completed = updated.length` and `failed = total - completed`, but if `updateMany` can partially succeed, this won't accurately reflect the actual success/failure breakdown.

Additionally, if `updateMany` throws an exception after partial success, the progress won't reflect the partial completion before the error.
```suggestion
              // Treat returned items as successfully updated records
              completed = updated.length;
              failed = total - completed;
              emitProgress();
              return updated;
            } catch (error: unknown) {
              // If updateMany reports a bulk error with partial success, reflect that in progress
              const bulkError = error as {
                successCount?: number;
                failureCount?: number;
              };

              if (
                typeof bulkError?.successCount === 'number' ||
                typeof bulkError?.failureCount === 'number'
              ) {
                const successCount =
                  typeof bulkError.successCount === 'number'
                    ? bulkError.successCount
                    : undefined;
                const failureCount =
                  typeof bulkError.failureCount === 'number'
                    ? bulkError.failureCount
                    : (typeof successCount === 'number' ? total - successCount : undefined);

                if (typeof successCount === 'number') {
                  completed = successCount;
                }
                if (typeof failureCount === 'number') {
                  failed = failureCount;
                }

                emitProgress();

                // Re-throw so callers can inspect the bulk error details
                throw error;
              }

              // If updateMany is not supported or failed without partial counts,
              // fall back to individual updates. Silently fallback without logging.
```

The `attemptReconnect()` method uses recursion to retry connections, which could lead to a deep call stack if `maxReconnectAttempts` is set to a high value. While the default is 3, users can configure this to be higher (e.g., 10 or more), which could cause stack overflow issues in JavaScript environments with limited stack depth.

Consider refactoring to use an iterative approach (while loop) instead of recursion.
```suggestion
    let lastError: unknown = undefined;

    while (this.autoReconnect && this.reconnectAttempts < this.maxReconnectAttempts) {
      this.reconnectAttempts++;
      this.setConnectionState('reconnecting');

      // Exponential backoff: delay * 2^(attempts-1)
      const delay = this.reconnectDelay * Math.pow(2, this.reconnectAttempts - 1);
      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));

      this.connected = false;

      try {
        await this.client.connect();
        this.connected = true;
        this.reconnectAttempts = 0;
        this.setConnectionState('connected');
        return;
      } catch (error: unknown) {
        lastError = error;
        // Loop will continue if we still have attempts left
      }
    }

    const errorMessage =
      lastError instanceof Error ? lastError.message : 'Failed to reconnect to ObjectStack server';

    const connectionError = new ConnectionError(
      errorMessage,
      undefined,
      { originalError: lastError }
    );

    this.setConnectionState('error', connectionError);
    throw connectionError;
```
